# 14. What does `type="module"` actually do?

This is worth demonstrating.

Without:

```html
<script type="module">
```

try:

```html
<script src="app.js"></script>
```

You get an error similar to:

```text
Cannot use import statement outside a module
```

So:

```html
<script type="module">
```

tells the browser:

> Treat this JavaScript file as an ES Module.

---

# 15. Module scope vs normal script scope

Show:

### `a.js`

```js
const secret = "hello";
```

### `b.js`

```js
console.log(secret);
```

If loaded as normal scripts:

```html
<script src="a.js"></script>
<script src="b.js"></script>
```

It can access shared global scope in ways students may find surprising.

But modules:

```html
<script type="module" src="a.js"></script>
<script type="module" src="b.js"></script>
```

don't simply dump their variables into the global scope.

Explain:

> Modules have their own scope.

This is one reason modules are useful.

