# 18. `module.exports` vs `exports`

This is an important Node interview/practical concept.

Start:

```js
module.exports = {
    add,
    multiply
};
```

Then:

```js
exports.add = add;
exports.multiply = multiply;
```

Both work.

Explain:

```text
exports
   │
   └──── initially points to ────> module.exports
```

So this works:

```js
exports.add = add;
```

But this is dangerous:

```js
exports = {
    add
};
```

because you've reassigned the local `exports` variable rather than changing `module.exports`.

> Prefer `module.exports` when exporting an entire value/object because it makes the actual mechanism clearer.

---

# 19. CommonJS vs ES Modules


| CommonJS | ES Modules |
|---|---|
| `require()` | `import` |
| `module.exports` | `export` |
| `exports.foo` | `export const foo` |
| Traditionally Node | Standard JavaScript module system |
| Synchronous `require` semantics | Static module syntax |

> Modern Node supports ES Modules too.

So Node isn't restricted to CommonJS.

